[Music]  2018 was a difficult year to sum up at  times it felt chaotic at times it felt  surreal at times it felt strangely calm  and in the world of video games the same  rang true it was a year of sleeper hits  and astounding disappointments as old  heroes returned juggernauts began their  streak and veteran studios took major  missteps the news surrounding games was  just as varied with developer  acquisitions proceeding massive studio  closures conversations arose about  working conditions and harmful company  cultures and each new day kept us on our  toes that's why I waited for 2018 to  officially be over before making this  video it was one of those years that you  can only see clearly in the rearview  mirror right off the bat Monster Hunter  world transformed Capcom's cult  role-playing series into a commercial  hit the newest entry brought the  franchise to Sony and Microsoft consoles  as well as PC selling 11 million copies  as of last fall it's now Capcom's best  selling game ever stealing the crown  from the long reigning Resident Evil 5  and who among us doesn't remember  playing God of War slowly realizing that  Santa Monica studio had taken one of  videogames most generic characters and  given him profound depth God of War  would go on to win numerous Game of the  Year accolades at IGN polygons and the  game awards show marking a turning point  for a franchise that a few years ago was  all but dead  speaking of redeemed characters 2018  also saw the return of Marvel's web  slinging superhero as insomniac  spider-man accrued nominations and  awards it drew comparisons to 2004 as  revered spider-man 2 with thrilling  movement mechanics frenetic combat and a  surprisingly gripping story it marked a  renaissance for the Marvel character but  also the developer itself targeting back  to its halcyon days with ratchet &amp; clank  resistance and spyro and as one last  note on spider-man it's also the fastest  selling PlayStation exclusive of all  time staying in the realm of Sales  veteran developer Rockstar Games  released its long-awaited Red Dead  Redemption - and  as is customary with the studios titles  sauce sales of ludicrous proportions  it sold 17 million copies to customers  and retailers in its first 12 days and  outsold the lifetime sales of the first  Red Dead Redemption an 8 year old game  in its first 8 days but Red Dead 2 was  risky in terms of design rockstars  newest release forces you to take your  time plan your travels and move at a  slow deliberate pace as westerns go it's  more of a Lonesome Dove than a 3:10 to  Yuma and in GameSpot's humble correct  opinion this approach paid off the  aforementioned games all succeeded in  one way or another be it critical  adoration or commercial records but 2018  was not without its triple-a missteps in  March I conic developer rare released  its multiplayer games sea of thieves and  while its premise had promised it soon  became clear that most of its appeal  rested on the surface see if thieves has  its advocates and rare has work to  expand it after its release but by and  large its bare-bones design made it an  afterthought as the months passed a  similar scenario occurred later in the  year as bethesda game studios made its  own pivot into the multiplayer sphere  fallout 76 released with an array of  boring quests a lifeless world and a  slew of bugs - boo many players lost  their save files completely within two  weeks of its release fallout 76 had  drawn the ire of redditors Steam users  and longtime Bethesda fans alike for all  the ups and downs of the triple-a  release cycle 2018 was defined just as  much if not more by its phenomenal indie  lineup in january developers matt  Thorsten and noel bairey along with a  small team released celeste  the demanding 2d platformer that doubles  as a parable about living with mental  health like the best games of its genre  it expertly introduces new mechanics  throughout the play through morphing a  simple premise into a complex video game  shortly thereafter subset games released  into the breach it plays like a  dangerous game of chess strewn with  explosives hulking mechs and vicious  kaiju across several locales it's a  stunning display of effective  simplicity that remains exciting after  hundreds of hours after about a year in  early access developer motion twin  released its roguelike darling dead  cells to critical acclaim its seamless  blend of 2d combat Metroidvania  exploration and RPG weapon progression  made it one of the year's best releases  it's one of those games that's rewarding  every step of the way several months  after return of the oh burden became the  sleeper hit among the rush of holiday  releases created by papers please  developer Lucas Pope oh burden places  players in the shoes of an insurance  agent investigating the disappearance of  the titular ship's crew with  supernatural elements a fragmented  narrative and clever time travel  abilities it's being lauded as one of  the best detective games to date as for  one of the year's best games that didn't  actually release in 2018 hallow night  made its way to Nintendo switch drawing  far more attention than its 2017 PC  release it combines an ominous  foreboding world with challenging combat  and a refusal to hold players hands it  was one of several titles that found new  life on Nintendo's portable console it's  funny because Nintendo was actually  criticized for its plethora of  third-party releases many accused the  company of padding an otherwise quiet  year outside of Pokemon let's go super  mario party mario tennis aces and super  smash bros ultimate  but to me Nintendo's third-party lineup  was one of 2018 s bright spots it not  only let me play some of my favorite  games on the go but it also rekindled my  love for games I had already poured  dozens of hours into Microsoft's  exclusive portfolio was once again  sparse with the excellent Forza horizon  4 and the underrated state of decay -  picking up much of the slack left by sea  of thieves but upon taking a closer look  it's clear that Microsoft actually had a  great year it released its new adaptive  controller allowing passionate fans with  disabilities to further enjoy the games  they loved it made good on its promise  of more backwards compatibility adding  numerous classics to the Xbox one's  library it also brought in millions of  players into its Netflix like  subscription service game pass and  finally Microsoft made several massive  studio acquisitions adding veterans such  as obsidian ninja theory and undead labs  to its first party team Sony on the  other hand relied almost entirely on its  exclusives the success of spider-man and  God of War aside ps4 also served as a  home for ninokuni to Detroit become  human  Yakuza 6 and Yakuza kiwami to glue point  games also gave shadow the colossus a  fantastic remaster bringing the classic  back to gorgeous life and of course this  list would not be complete without  Tetris effect developers resin Aaron  Monstars revived the legendary title to  universal acclaim  it's a dazzling display of sight sound  and euphoric sensory overload and it's  all the more effective and V are in fact  2018 was strewn with excellent VR titles  Tetris effect drew the most attention  but we also had Maus firewall zero our  beat saber and the astonishing Astro bot  rescue mission as opposed to previous  year's VR developers seemed less  concerned with releasing proofs of  concept and more focused on just  releasing incredible games I don't think  it's bold to say that 2018 was the best  year for VR to date  [Music]  for all of the news that emerged 2018  was also a sobering reminder that there  are thousands of people behind the games  we play in August  Kotaku published an article about the  sexist culture at League of Legends  developer Riot Games reporter Cecilia  Don Stassi Oh spoke to 28 current and  former employees whose accounts painted  the picture of an environment hostile  toward women  the phrase Bro culture cropped up on  several different occasions women often  felt degraded and objectified and  numerous cases of alleged sexual  misconduct came out in these interviews  following the report riot claimed it  would double its efforts to investigate  claims of harassment in the workplace  but in December when the company  suspended CEO of Scott Gelb for two  months of unpaid leave many Rai  employees felt the punishment was too  soft Gelb was said to regularly harassed  employees perpetuating a fraternity  mindset based around bathroom humor and  it's alleged efforts to right the ship  riot still clearly has problems to  address and in the case of rockstar  games the conversation about working  conditions took on a very different form  in October New York magazine published a  feature about the making of Red Dead  Redemption to the publication spoke at  length with Dan Hauser  rockstars co-founder and lead writer  about the effort put into the massive  Western in a now infamous quote Houser  said the team spent multiple hundred  hour weeks to bring Red Dead 2 to  fruition Houser later clarified he was  only referring to the core writers in  this quote and after speaking with  dozens of former and current Rockstar  members Kotaku's Jason Schreyer  published a story about this culture of  crunch the reports as Schreyer wrote  were complicated and sometimes  contradictory many Rockstar employees  didn't work overtime others did but  found it to be rewarding others still  said the job cost them their closest  relationships and that the studio  fostered a mentality in which weekends  and nights were expected of them  rockstar studio culture became the focus  of the ongoing discussion about the  ethics of crunch and of course the  discussion did not end with red Dead's  gargantuan release in the wake of the  game success questions arose regarding  how much of the games affecting  narrative  an obsessive amount of detail were a  result of overtime is crunch necessary  to make games of this nature will things  change in the future how many other  Triple A Studios might have practices  like this that we have no idea about  2018 was a reminder that these questions  and the stories of numerous employees  with diverse backgrounds are just as  important as the games themselves we  witnessed the end of telltale games in  September reports of poor management and  that same pervasive crunch emerged 250  employees showed up for work one day and  only hours later discovered they have  lost their jobs  crunch culture may not have been the  only cause but it was a cause  nonetheless it's easy to remember 2018  as a grim year for every game that  elevated the medium just as much bad  news seemed to bring it back down but in  moving forward it's important to  recognize the bright spots as well not  just the examples of what not to do but  also the ones that show us how things  can be done French developer motion twin  creator of indie darling dead cells  stresses work-life balance as its main  pillar it has no seniority it has no  executives each member makes the same  salary as the next and of course the  studio also made one of 2018 s best  games motion twin is much smaller than  the Triple A companies with problematic  deep-rooted working conditions but  regardless dead cells is a challenge to  the rest of the games industry it's  proof that success doesn't require  crunch and rigid hierarchies it's an  example of a healthy studio making a  stellar piece of entertainment elsewhere  in the games industry hope endured in  the wake of Telltale's closing numerous  developers rallied to support the  affected employees Ubisoft hosted a job  fair in the Bay Area Naughty Dog's Sony  bend and Santa Monica studio shared  their job listings as well as Firaxis  games continues to work on its next  project  creative director Jake Solomon espoused  Telltale's impact on the medium and he  also shared job listings of his own  above all 2018 was a massive reminder  that people make games that people play  them and that people share them and it's  worth looking back in order to move  forward so here's two more improvements  in 2019  another year of video games  [Music]

